{
  "id": "choro",
  "name": "Choro",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Brazilian instrumental urban-popular-music tradition originating 1870s-onward in Rio de Janeiro from European salon-dance (polka, schottische, waltz, mazurka) re-rhythmicized through Afro-Brazilian syncopation (especially habanera-derived). The canonical regional-choro ensemble: solo lead (flute, clarinet, or mandolin), seven-string guitar (violão-de-sete) providing baixaria walking-bass-line, six-string guitar accompanying-harmony, cavaquinho four-double-course soprano-guitar rhythmic-strum, and pandeiro frame-drum-with-jingles. The repertoire layers virtuosic compositional pieces (often modulating through multiple keys with rapid 16th-note arpeggios) with roda-de-choro participatory jam-session-tradition. Three historical phases: foundational late-19th-century, golden-age 1920s-1940s, and bossa-nova-and-modern-revival 1950s-onward.",
